```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <sstream>

std::string markdownToHtml(const std::string& markdown) {
    // Create a string stream to hold the HTML output
    std::stringstream htmlStream;

    // Write the markdown text to the string stream
    htmlStream << markdown;

    // Return the HTML string
    return htmlStream.str();
}

int main() {
    std::string markdown = "This is a sample markdown text";
    std::string html = markdownToHtml(markdown);
    std::cout << "Markdown: " << markdown << std::endl;
    std::cout << "HTML: " << html << std::endl;
    return 0;
}
```

This function uses a `std::stringstream` to convert the markdown text to HTML. The `std::stringstream` class allows us to write the markdown text to a stream, which is then converted back to a string using the `str()` method.

Note that this function assumes that the markdown text is well-formed and does not contain any HTML tags or special characters that would affect the conversion. If you need to handle more complex markdown text, you may need to use a more sophisticated parser or library to handle the conversion.

